preConditionHook:
// THIS BAR IS AT LEAST AS BIG AS THE SESSION'S AVERAGE BAR SO FAR. Brooks's
// gate on range scalps: a tight trading range is only worth fading when the
// bars inside it are large enough to pay for the trade.
//
// The window is what makes this its own rule, and it is the same growing
// session-to-date window SessionAvgBarRangeStop uses for a money stop -- one
// shared primitive, read here as an entry filter on the CURRENT bar instead
// of as a stop distance. Not BarRangeAboveStd, whose benchmark is a FIXED
// N-bar mean plus K standard deviations, and not BarRangeAtrMultiple, whose
// benchmark is a fixed rolling ATR: this benchmark is one bar wide at the
// open and grows through the day, so no lookback setting reproduces it.
//
// The average INCLUDES the current bar, exactly as the pseudocode's
// average(high - low, barsSinceSessionOpen) does, so on the session's first
// bar the test is high - low >= sizeMultiple * (high - low) and fires at
// sizeMultiple <= 1. That is deliberate: the first bar of a session has no
// other bar to be compared against.
//
// It is a mean of the bar's high minus its low, not of true range, and not
// the WFSafe_AvgTrueRange rolling accumulator -- there is no seeding and no
// length-change behaviour here, just a running sum reset at the open.
//
// barsIn is at least 1 on every evaluated bar (the session branch sets it to
// 1 and the other increments from 0), so the division is safe.
//
// Direction-agnostic: the size of a bar is the same fact for a buyer and a
// seller, so both conditions are identical text.
//
// SESSION MODEL, per the standing decision: the twin's date > date[1],
// mirrored as day_of(ctx.Time(0)) > day_of(ctx.Time(1)). Intraday bars only.
//
// Deepest bar touched: 1.
if (day_of(ctx.Time(0)) > day_of(ctx.Time(1))) {
    barsIn = 1;
    rangeSum = high[0] - low[0];
} else {
    barsIn = barsIn + 1;
    rangeSum = rangeSum + (high[0] - low[0]);
}
avgBarRange = rangeSum / barsIn;
